# Only the files that state or enforce the contract have an owner: the proof
# root, the two records the audit compares it with, the audit and its mutation
# tests, the toolchain and build configuration, the C and Rust sides of the
# ABI, and this directory. Every other file, including every Lean module under
# lean/Tamarin and lean/TamarinRuntime and lean/TamarinProposers.lean, has no
# owner and can be changed under the gate alone.
#
# The exports are in two classes, both listed in lean/ExportManifest.txt.
#   * A contract export (`export` lines, eleven of them) is one whose reply
#     the Rust backend uses as the answer. What is promised about it is
#     exactly the theorems of lean/TamarinCI.lean that mention it.
#   * A proposer export (`proposer` lines) has no contract. The Rust backend
#     uses its output only inside a proposed family of variants, and uses that
#     family only after the contract export tamarin_session_variant_check
#     accepted it. A wrong proposal is rejected there, so nothing needs to be
#     promised about a proposer and its algorithm can be changed arbitrarily.
#     Moving an export between the classes changes what is trusted and is an
#     owner decision: the manifest and the proof root have an owner.
# lean/TamarinProposers.lean holds the theorems about proposers that used to
# be contract statements, the coverage statements that were stated through
# reference procedures, and library facts about no export. CI builds it so the
# proofs stay alive. None of it is promised to a caller, which is sound for
# the reason above: those results reach the prover only through the checker.
#
# The Rust declarations of the C bridge and of the Lean exports that take
# machine words only are generated by build.rs from native/lean_unification.c
# and from the declarations Lean emitted; the bridge is compiled against the
# latter. The Rust backend declares none by hand, so a call that disagrees with
# the bridge, or a bridge that disagrees with Lean, does not build.
#
# What the gate (the `lean-spec` job and, for local builds, build.rs) enforces
# on a change to an unowned file:
#   * every theorem of lean/TamarinCI.lean still elaborates, and CI replays
#     the whole environment in the kernel;
#   * lean/Tests/ExportAudit.lean, which reads the compiled modules from
#     outside, finds every export listed once as contract or proposer, the
#     kernel statement of every contract theorem, and every definition those
#     statements depend on (in whatever module it lives) equal to the records
#     in lean/ExportManifest.txt and lean/SpecManifest.txt, by SHA-256 of a
#     canonical encoding;
#   * no contract statement mentions a proposer, directly or through a
#     definition it depends on;
#   * no module, proposer code included, defines macros, syntax, elaborators
#     or initializers, imports `Lean`, replaces compiled code
#     (`implemented_by`, `extern`, unsafe, partial or opaque code behind an
#     export) or uses an unapproved axiom. Proposer code runs in the caller's
#     process, so it stays ordinary Lean although its results are not trusted.
# A change can therefore alter how an export computes, never what is proved
# about a contract export. Changing a definition that a contract statement
# depends on fails the audit until an owner regenerates lean/SpecManifest.txt;
# to optimise such a definition, add a faster copy and prove it equal.
#
# No contract statement names a search, a classifier or a reference
# procedure, so the unifier, the matcher, the table classifier, the reducers
# and the proposers can all be changed in place. The audit can follow a
# listed constant by type only (`contractTyped`); the list is empty.
#
# What the gate does not enforce: anything no theorem of lean/TamarinCI.lean
# states. That includes how often a contract export declines where no theorem
# limits it (certify_table beyond its class of constructor-pattern tables
# and three concrete packets), everything about a proposer (the generators
# general_variants, checked_variants and fold_variants are proposers),
# running time and memory, that the Rust backend really passes every
# proposed family through the checker, and the files under
# lean/Tests other than the two below, which are regression tests and can be
# edited like any implementation file.
